    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ramesses II ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for 66 years, the longest recorded reign of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, and built from the Delta to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> on a scale no earlier king had matched.</li><li>He claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> as a great victory, but the battle is generally considered a stalemate, and the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> ended in Year 21 in a treaty of which both versions survive.</li><li>His monuments served as propaganda for his victories and his divinity, and he was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> worshipped as a god in his own lifetime.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In September 1976 the mummy of Ramesses II was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for treatment and received at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>–Le Bourget Airport with full military honours befitting a king. The persistent claim that it was issued with a passport for the journey is incorrect.</p></aside>
    </section>

