    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1303 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Ramesses is born, the grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and officer who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> as Ramesses I</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1279 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He succeeds his father Seti I as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> at the age of 25</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1274 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He fights the Hittite forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, a battle generally considered a stalemate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1272 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, his armies take Moab and recapture Upi, the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1259 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He concludes the peace known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kadesh">Treaty of Kadesh</a> with the Hittite king Ḫattušili III</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1255 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">With his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a> he travels into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> to inaugurate the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1213 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He dies aged about 90, early in his 67th regnal year, and is succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/horemheb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horemheb.jpg" alt="Horemheb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StatueOfHoremhebAndTheGodHorus-DetailOfHoremheb01_KunsthistorischesMuseum_Nov13-10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Captmondo, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ramesses II was not born a prince. His grandfather, Ramesses I, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and military officer under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, who appointed him as his successor; Ramesses II was then about eleven years old. When Ramesses I died, his son Seti I became king, and at about the age of fourteen Ramesses II was designated prince regent. The family came from the far north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> rather than from the south, as the Twelfth, Seventeenth and Eighteenth Dynasties had, a point the Egyptologist Frank J. Yurco drew from the features of the king's mummy. It also had ties to the god Set: Seti's name means "follower of Set", and Ramesses I had served as High Priest of Set under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>.</p><p>The Egypt he grew up in was that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the most powerful period of ancient Egypt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, south of the first cataract of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, had been a colony for 200 years, but territories once held had been lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. Seti I had made a triumphal entry into the Syrian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> about a decade before his son fought there, and he kept a summer palace in the eastern Delta on the site his son later made a capital. Ramesses may have been present on his father's Libyan campaigns, to which records such as the Aswan Stele of his second year perhaps hark back. When he was about 22, two of his own sons, including Amun-her-khepeshef, accompanied him on at least one campaign in Nubia; reliefs at Beit el-Wali show him charging tribes south of Egypt in a war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, with his young sons behind him in chariots of their own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses.jpg" alt="Pi-Ramesses" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolossalstatue_Qantir.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Iri-en-achti, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ramesses came to the throne on a day recorded as III Shemu, day 27, which most Egyptologists take to be 31 May 1279 BCE; he was 25. In the early part of the reign he gave his attention to building cities, temples and monuments, and in his third year he began what has been called the most ambitious building project since the pyramids, raised almost 1,500 years earlier. He also set out to recover lost territories and to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s borders. The Egyptian army of his reign is estimated at some 100,000 men.</p><p>In his second year he defeated the Sherden sea <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, who had been attacking cargo vessels on the sea routes to Egypt. He posted troops and ships along the coast, let the pirates attack what they took for prey, and captured them in a single action, probably near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>; the Lukka and the Shekelesh were defeated in the same battle. Many Sherden, with horned helmets and round shields, appear soon afterwards in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s bodyguard.</p><p>In the fourth year he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, set up the first of the stelae at Nahr el-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Kalb</a> near what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and captured Amurru, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. He founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, in the eastern Delta, possibly to be closer to his territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and Syria, though his motives are uncertain. Its workshops are supposed to have produced some 1,000 weapons in a week, about 250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> in two weeks and 1,000 shields in a week and a half. From there he moved against the Hittite Empire, a more substantial enemy than any he had yet faced.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="Abu Simbel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramsis,_Aswan_Governorate,_Egypt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">youssef_alam, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, in his fifth year, was fought against the Hittite forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>. After a month's advance through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, according to the Egyptian sources, Ramesses reached Kadesh on 1 May 1274 BCE. His troops were caught in an ambush: Hittite chariotry broke through his second division and attacked his camp. Reinforced by other divisions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> counterattacked and routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, whose survivors swam the Orontes to the city walls. Unable to sustain a long siege, Ramesses returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Late in his seventh year he went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. One force, under his son Amun-her-khepeshef, took Edom-Seir and Moab; the other, under the king, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, and the reunited army recaptured Upi, the land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In his eighth and ninth years he pushed north into Amurru and besieged and took Dapur, where no Egyptian soldier had been seen since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, almost 120 years earlier. In Year 21 (1259 BCE) he concluded with the Hittite king Ḫattušili III the peace known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kadesh">Treaty of Kadesh</a>, whose 18 articles survive in an Egyptian and a Hittite version. No Egyptian campaigns in Canaan are mentioned after it.</p><p>He built from the Delta to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, "covering the land with buildings in a way no monarch before him had". Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, his largest works were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesseum">Ramesseum</a>, a mortuary temple in western Thebes with an enthroned statue of the king 17 metres high and weighing more than 1,000 tonnes, and the temple complex of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> in Nubia, which he and his queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a> travelled to inaugurate in 1255 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="Battle of Kadesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Abou_Simbel6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His chief opponents were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. Ramesses claimed a great victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a>, and he held the battlefield, but the battle is generally considered a stalemate and the Hittites the ultimate victors of the campaign: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> retreated, and Hittite forces invaded and briefly occupied the Egyptian possessions around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s sphere was reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, where princes, seemingly goaded on by the Hittites, began revolts. The northern conquests that followed proved ephemeral. The strip between Amurru and Kadesh returned to the Hittite fold within a year, so that he had to march on Dapur again in his tenth year, and that second success was as meaningless as the first, since neither power could decisively defeat the other.</p><p>The peace itself grew out of a crisis. Ḫattušili III had deposed his nephew Muršili III, who fled to Egypt; when Ḫattušili demanded his extradition, Ramesses denied any knowledge of his whereabouts, and the two empires came close to war. The two versions of the treaty disagree on one point: the Hittite text says the Egyptians came suing for peace, the Egyptian text the reverse.</p><p>His monuments carried his claims. He used art as propaganda for victories over foreigners, had the alleged rout of the Hittites at Kadesh repeated on temple walls, remodelled or usurped existing works, and displayed his cartouches even on buildings he did not construct. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> there are no detailed accounts of large campaigns, only generalised records of conquering and crushing, which may or may not refer to specific events.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merneptah.jpg" alt="Merneptah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_and_the_Pharaohs%27_Gold,_London_2026-03-06_-_149_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyspaosines, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 30th year of his reign Ramesses celebrated, by tradition, the Sed festival, a jubilee held to honour and rejuvenate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s strength. Such festivals were traditionally repeated every three years; Ramesses, who sometimes held them after two, celebrated thirteen or fourteen, more than any other pharaoh. As of Year 28 he favoured the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> above all other divinities, as two ostraca from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina">Deir el-Medina</a> show. With the northern border quiet after the treaty, his rule remained strong until his death.</p><p>By then, aged about 90, he suffered from severe dental problems, arthritis, hardening of the arteries and heart disease, and scans of the mummy indicate that he may have walked with a pronounced stoop. The cause of death is unknown; one possibility is the rupture of an abscess in his jaw, though researchers add that this cannot be determined with certainty. The last dated record of the reign is Year 67, first month of Akhet, day 18. A necropolis journal from Deir el-Medina records the "Sailing" of the king, a euphemism for his death, on the sixth day of the second month, which has been equated with 13 August 1213 BCE, after 66 years and 74 days on the throne; Jürgen von Beckerath placed the death in late July or early August, and Peter J. Brand in mid-July. Brand holds that he died in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, most likely at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>.</p><p>He was buried in tomb KV7 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>. Some of his estimated 52 sons, among them his first-born, Amun-her-khepeshef, are believed to have been buried in tomb KV5.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt.jpg" alt="Johann Ludwig Burckhardt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Ludwig_Burckhardt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> called him the "Great God" and prayed to enjoy his longevity; Ramesses IV asked the gods at Abydos to double for him the lifespan and reign of Ramesses II. He is often regarded as the greatest and most powerful pharaoh of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, though the dynasty waned after his death. Greek sources called him Ozymandias, from the first part of his regnal name, Usermaatre Setepenre; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> quotes an inscription, "King of Kings am I, Osymandias", which Percy Bysshe Shelley paraphrased in his poem "Ozymandias".</p><p>Because of looting, priests moved his body from KV7 to the tomb of queen Ahmose Inhapy and then to that of the high priest Pinedjem II; it was found in 1881 in tomb TT320. In 1976 the mummy was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for treatment and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in May 1977. A forensic study there by Pierre-Fernand Ceccaldi concluded from its red hair and cranial features that the king was of a "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> type"; Cheikh Anta Diop disputed the result, arguing that hair morphology cannot determine the ethnicity of a mummy. In April 2021 the mummy was moved to the National Museum of Egyptian Civilization in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in the Pharaohs' Golden Parade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>, its facade almost covered by sand, was found in 1813 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</a>.</p><p>Films and popular media often cast him as the pharaoh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a>. Scholars generally do not recognise the biblical account as a historical event, and Egyptologists dispute the identification; the historian Lester L. Grabbe writes that an Egypt devastated by plagues is not compatible with everything known about this ruler.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ramesses II ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1279 to 1213 BCE, the longest recorded reign of any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a>, made peace with them in his twenty-first year, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesseum">Ramesseum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>. Later kings prayed for his longevity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> remembered him as Ozymandias.</em></p>

